# Shell scripts must keep LF endings, whatever the checkout platform.
#
# ops/entrypoint-app.sh is COPIED INTO the application image and
# ops/backup-loop.sh is bind-mounted and run by the backup container. A
# CRLF checkout gives them a "#!/bin/sh\r" shebang, so the kernel looks
# for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r" and the container dies
# with "no such file or directory" — a failure that says nothing about
# its cause. Windows checkouts with core.autocrlf=true do exactly this.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else read by a Linux tool as text.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
